    "result": "I've successfully fixed the bug in the dates_eom.py test where invoices with 'eom' (end of month) payment terms issued in February of a leap year were getting the wrong due date.\n\n## Summary of Changes\n\n**Problem**: The `end_of_month` function was hardcoding February to 28 days regardless of whether it was a leap year, causing incorrect due dates for leap year February invoices.\n\n**Solution**: Updated the `end_of_month` function in `invoicing/dates.py` to properly detect leap years using `calendar.isleap()` and `calendar.monthrange()`, so February correctly has 29 days in leap years (like 2024) and 28 days otherwise.\n\n**Verification**: \n- \u2705 Both failing tests now pass\n- \u2705 All 85 tests in the full test suite continue to pass\n- \u2705 Fix works for all years, not just 2024\n- \u2705 No regressions introduced\n\nThe fix is general and robust, properly handling leap years while maintaining all existing functionality.",
